{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"bbaafe8e1aaf2b9a","type":"group","x":-120,"y":-180,"width":1580,"height":5000,"color":"5","label":"1 · RECON"},
		{"id":"f0dc7a2a3e96b42f","type":"group","x":1680,"y":-180,"width":1580,"height":5000,"color":"5","label":"2 · ENUMERATION"},
		{"id":"76bf3b67cc7a67c4","type":"group","x":3480,"y":-180,"width":1580,"height":5000,"color":"4","label":"3 · INITIAL ACCESS"},
		{"id":"c4edfa32c2173cb3","type":"group","x":5280,"y":-180,"width":1580,"height":5000,"color":"3","label":"4 · PRIVILEGE ESCALATION"},
		{"id":"0f4f5140ba1058a9","type":"group","x":7080,"y":-180,"width":1580,"height":5000,"color":"2","label":"5 · CREDENTIALS"},
		{"id":"a63f27f3a7b06d54","type":"group","x":8880,"y":-180,"width":1580,"height":5000,"color":"6","label":"6 · PIVOTING"},
		{"id":"c2c3b841c50769c6","type":"group","x":10680,"y":-180,"width":1580,"height":5000,"color":"1","label":"7 · ACTIVE DIRECTORY"},
		{"id":"784c65adac5c13da","type":"group","x":12480,"y":-180,"width":1580,"height":5000,"color":"4","label":"8 · PROOF"},
		{"id":"a965581a2082c74d","type":"text","text":"# 3. INITIAL ACCESS\n\n**Goal:** obtain a shell or authenticated execution context.\n\nUse the *best-supported* path from enumeration; do not force one technique when another service gives cleaner access.","x":3600,"y":0,"width":1250,"height":460,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"7a0476b126a75c02","type":"text","text":"## Web exploitation paths\n\n- Directory traversal → files/keys/configs\n- LFI / PHP wrappers / log poisoning\n- RFI\n- File upload → webshell / key overwrite\n- Command injection\n- SQLi → data → creds → `xp_cmdshell` / `INTO OUTFILE` when applicable\n- XSS only when it advances access/privilege\n- API authorization/property abuse","x":3600,"y":560,"width":1250,"height":760},
		{"id":"6003953a78462859","type":"text","text":"## Authentication / password paths\n\n- Default/weak/reused credentials\n- Targeted Hydra / form attacks\n- SSH key passphrase cracking\n- Password spraying **only after lockout awareness**\n- NTLM/Net-NTLM opportunities\n\nValidate credentials against likely services, not everywhere blindly.","x":3600,"y":1400,"width":1250,"height":670},
		{"id":"8189b1e4852b8409","type":"text","text":"# 2. ENUMERATION\n\n**Goal:** turn ports into concrete attack hypotheses.\n\nFor every service ask: **What is it? Version? Auth? Hidden content? Users? Misconfiguration? Known vulnerability?**","x":1800,"y":0,"width":1250,"height":460,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"097e737cfc8d0b2c","type":"text","text":"## Common non-web branches\n\n**SMB:** shares, users, OS/domain, anonymous/guest, writable shares.\n\n**SMTP:** banner, VRFY/user discovery, mail paths.\n\n**SNMP:** communities, users, processes, software, local ports.\n\n**SSH/RDP/WinRM/DB:** auth surface + versions.\n\n**DNS:** names + domain structure.","x":1800,"y":2150,"width":1250,"height":760},
		{"id":"1fa0490e22261e21","type":"text","text":"## Vulnerability research\n\n```bash\nsearchsploit <product> <version>\nnmap -sV -p <port> --script vuln <IP>\n```\n\nAlso: Exploit-DB, GitHub, Packet Storm, NSE.\n\n**Read the PoC:** target version, auth requirement, OS/arch, callback IP/port, paths, dependencies, shellcode, Python version.","x":1800,"y":2990,"width":1250,"height":720},
		{"id":"7ad89d3613b760b7","type":"text","text":"## Public exploit path\n\n1. Match exact product/version.\n2. Read exploit completely.\n3. Identify assumptions.\n4. Copy locally and edit callback/target/paths.\n5. Compile/fix dependencies if needed.\n6. Test the smallest change.\n7. Confirm command execution before upgrading to a shell.\n\nChapter 14 = **fix the exploit, not the target.**","x":3600,"y":2150,"width":1250,"height":780},
		{"id":"44a6208e0324c304","type":"text","text":"## Client-side / phishing path\n\nWhen the scenario gives a human interaction path:\n- fingerprint target/client\n- Office macro / document technique\n- Windows Library / shortcut\n- host payload + listener\n- craft believable delivery\n\nChapter 24 uses phishing to obtain an **internal foothold** after public compromise.","x":3600,"y":3010,"width":1250,"height":700},
		{"id":"12c821375ebcba06","type":"text","text":"# Decision: shell / authenticated execution?\n\n**YES:** stabilize if needed, record `whoami` + `hostname`, then local enumeration.\n\n**NO:** inspect error/output, modify one assumption, or return to enumeration and choose another path.","x":3600,"y":3630,"width":1250,"height":640,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"9f8e2c3ec072bd0c","type":"text","text":"# 8. PROOF\n\n**Goal:** prove control clearly and preserve reproducible evidence.\n\nStandalone: root/SYSTEM + proof artifact.\nAD: privileged control of required hosts/DC + proof.","x":12600,"y":0,"width":1250,"height":460,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"7fccb85cc1458f80","type":"text","text":"## Service triage\n\nFor **each open port**:\n1. Identify product/version.\n2. Connect manually if possible.\n3. Check default/anonymous access.\n4. Enumerate users/resources.\n5. Search exact version + feature + vulnerability.\n6. Validate before exploitation.\n\nScanner finding ≠ confirmed exploit.","x":1800,"y":560,"width":1250,"height":650},
		{"id":"3be3ddeeb4836948","type":"text","text":"## HTTP / HTTPS branch\n\n- Nmap HTTP NSE / headers\n- Wappalyzer / WhatWeb\n- Gobuster directories/files/vhosts\n- `robots.txt`, source, JS, sitemaps\n- Burp Proxy + Repeater\n- API paths/methods/properties\n- CMS/plugin enumeration (e.g. WPScan)\n\nThen test **input handling + auth + file paths + uploads + server-side execution**.","x":1800,"y":1290,"width":1250,"height":780},
		{"id":"00c0dce0588334c3","type":"text","text":"## Verify exact context\n\nLinux:\n```bash\nwhoami\nid\nhostname\nip a\n```\n\nWindows:\n```text\nwhoami\nhostname\nipconfig\n```\n\nFor AD, verify the **specific host/DC** and privilege required by the objective.","x":12600,"y":560,"width":1250,"height":720},
		{"id":"3d534afff5a78869","type":"text","text":"## Capture proof artifacts\n\n- Read the required `local.txt` / `proof.txt` (or current exam-required artifact).\n- Preserve terminal output showing **identity + hostname + proof** together when practical.\n- Record the exact commands and credentials/path used.\n- Keep screenshots/evidence legible and reproducible.","x":12600,"y":1360,"width":1250,"height":720},
		{"id":"c5ce08d451657349","type":"text","text":"# 7. ACTIVE DIRECTORY\n\n**Goal:** map identities/relationships and chain credentials + permissions + sessions into higher privilege, ideally DC control for an AD set.","x":10800,"y":0,"width":1250,"height":460,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"0b76f8a86e59cf49","type":"text","text":"## Establish domain context\n\n```text\nwhoami /all\nhostname\nipconfig /all\nnet user /domain\nnet group /domain\n```\n\nIdentify domain, DC/DNS, current user/groups, accessible shares, and whether the host is domain joined.","x":10800,"y":560,"width":1250,"height":690},
		{"id":"389dd48f40d4a1c5","type":"text","text":"## Authentication attacks\n\n- check lockout/password policy first\n- targeted password spray\n- AS-REP roasting\n- Kerberoasting\n- crack only what is crackable/useful\n- reuse NTLM/Kerberos where possible\n\nMap every credential to **where it is admin / logged in / has ACL rights**.","x":10800,"y":2200,"width":1250,"height":720},
		{"id":"6fd3eeaedfb4a44c","type":"text","text":"## Lateral movement\n\nPlaintext/admin creds:\n- WMI / WinRM / WinRS\n- PsExec\n\nHashes/tickets:\n- Pass-the-Hash\n- Overpass-the-Hash\n- Pass-the-Ticket\n\nOther: DCOM\n\nAfter landing on a new host: **enumerate locally + dump only the credentials/tickets your privilege allows.**","x":10800,"y":3000,"width":1250,"height":760},
		{"id":"da711f610a7c4cf1","type":"text","text":"## Toward domain compromise\n\n- LSASS credentials / tickets when privileged\n- service-account attack paths\n- Silver Ticket when service hash + SID fit the scenario\n- DCSync when replication rights are obtained\n- Golden Ticket / NTDS shadow-copy techniques are persistence/credential-recovery concepts from Ch.23\n\nFor OSCP: focus on the path that proves the required compromise.","x":10800,"y":3840,"width":1250,"height":820},
		{"id":"9c2c96dd72db91c8","type":"text","text":"# Decision: required domain privilege / DC access?\n\n**NO:** rerun BloodHound/manual enum with new identity, test newly exposed sessions/ACLs/SPNs/hosts, and loop through Credentials + Lateral Movement.\n\n**YES:** collect Proof.","x":10800,"y":3650,"width":1250,"height":620,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"969b9a5a7f976de2","type":"text","text":"## Reconstruct the attack chain\n\nFor each host, write:\n1. Recon finding\n2. Enumeration evidence\n3. Initial access method\n4. Privilege escalation\n5. Credentials obtained\n6. Pivot/lateral movement\n7. Final privilege + proof\n\nThis makes the report much easier than reconstructing it later.","x":12600,"y":2160,"width":1250,"height":720},
		{"id":"ca14919dd7444e1c","type":"text","text":"# Host complete ≠ assessment complete\n\nIf more targets/AD objectives remain, feed the new facts back into the top loop:\n\n**new credential → old host**\n**new route → new subnet**\n**new AD identity → new BloodHound path**\n**new privilege → new credential sources**","x":12600,"y":2960,"width":1250,"height":690,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"f3263293793134ea","type":"text","text":"### PEN-200 source\n\n[[PEN200_Chapter_24_Assembling_the_Pieces_OSCP_Notes]]\n\nChapter 24’s final pattern:\n**Nmap/Gobuster/WhatWeb/WPScan → file read → SSH → Linux PE → credentials → phishing → internal enum → pivot → Kerberoast/relay → LSASS → PtH/PsExec → DC proof.**","x":12600,"y":3730,"width":1250,"height":690,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"bcd6b11797a64b66","type":"text","text":"# 6. PIVOTING\n\n**Goal:** reach networks/services that Kali cannot access directly.\n\nFirst identify interfaces, routes, DNS, and localhost-only services; then choose the simplest tunnel.","x":9000,"y":0,"width":1250,"height":460,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"518c1aff54574c07","type":"text","text":"## Hashes / keys / cracking\n\n- `ssh2john` / `keepass2john` → John\n- Hashcat mode identification + wordlists/rules\n- NTLM cracking or **Pass-the-Hash** when accepted\n- Net-NTLMv2 capture → crack or relay\n\nAsk: **Do I need the plaintext, or can I reuse the hash/ticket directly?**","x":7200,"y":1420,"width":1250,"height":720},
		{"id":"e2ba7a74a3e1733f","type":"text","text":"## Credential matrix / reuse test\n\nTrack:\n`DOMAIN\\user | password/hash | source | tested hosts | services | privilege`\n\nValidate selectively against:\n- SMB\n- WinRM/RDP/WMI\n- SSH\n- web/CMS\n- databases\n\nNew credentials often unlock a host you already enumerated.","x":7200,"y":2220,"width":1250,"height":730},
		{"id":"71bdc039c050831b","type":"text","text":"## Detect pivot opportunity\n\nOn compromised host check:\n- interfaces / IPs\n- route table\n- DNS servers / suffix\n- ARP/neighbors\n- listening services\n- hostname patterns\n\nA second NIC or route to RFC1918 space = **new recon scope**.","x":9000,"y":560,"width":1250,"height":680},
		{"id":"f77f8cc3975c522d","type":"text","text":"## Choose the simplest tunnel\n\n**One service:** local/remote port forward or Socat.\n\n**Many TCP services:** SOCKS + Proxychains.\n\n**Whole subnet / Linux pivot:** sshuttle if feasible.\n\n**Windows:** `ssh.exe`, Plink, Netsh.\n\nPrefer a stable, understandable path over the fanciest tunnel.","x":9000,"y":1320,"width":1250,"height":730},
		{"id":"af3ab9d84f04c896","type":"text","text":"## SSH / SOCKS toolbox\n\n- SSH local forward\n- SSH dynamic forward (`-D`)\n- SSH remote forward (`-R`)\n- remote dynamic SOCKS\n- Proxychains\n- `sshuttle`\n- Socat\n\nFor Nmap through SOCKS, use **TCP connect** (`-sT`) rather than raw SYN.","x":9000,"y":2130,"width":1250,"height":700},
		{"id":"6025482f66addb6d","type":"text","text":"## Restricted egress / DPI\n\n**Chisel:** HTTP/WebSocket tunnel, reverse SOCKS, browser-friendly forwards.\n\n**dnscat2:** DNS tunnel when DNS is the viable egress path.\n\n**Metasploit:** `autoroute` + SOCKS proxy / Meterpreter port forwards.\n\nUse only when ordinary SSH/forwarding is blocked or inconvenient.","x":9000,"y":2910,"width":1250,"height":720},
		{"id":"cbcf4a91ba6279f3","type":"text","text":"# RE-ENUMERATE THE INTERNAL NETWORK\n\n- host discovery where possible\n- TCP services through tunnel\n- SMB/DNS/HTTP/internal apps\n- AD/domain clues\n- sessions and service accounts\n\nTreat every internal host like a new target IP, but correlate it with credentials and AD data already collected.","x":9000,"y":3710,"width":1250,"height":720,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"97bf33f9c97621a4","type":"text","text":"## Enumerate relationships — repeat with every identity\n\n- users / groups / computers / OS\n- Domain Admins\n- SPNs / service accounts\n- sessions\n- where current user is local admin\n- shares + SYSVOL\n- ACLs / GenericAll-like rights\n- PowerView\n- SharpHound → BloodHound shortest paths\n\n**AD is a graph, not a list of machines.**","x":10800,"y":1330,"width":1250,"height":790},
		{"id":"0a9f0a61ca1f8cb6","type":"text","text":"# 4. PRIVILEGE ESCALATION\n\n**Goal:** low privilege → `root`, `SYSTEM`, or useful admin rights.\n\nImmediately enumerate locally after foothold. Manual checks first; automation is a cross-check.","x":5400,"y":0,"width":1250,"height":460,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"221cb81c37cfbaf5","type":"text","text":"## First 2 minutes after foothold\n\n```text\nwhoami / id\nhostname\nipconfig / ip a\nroutes\nlistening ports\nprocesses\nusers/groups\n```\n\nThen determine **Linux vs Windows**, privilege/token/group context, and whether the host has a second network interface.","x":5400,"y":560,"width":1250,"height":670},
		{"id":"b2911b4ef9400462","type":"text","text":"## Linux local enumeration\n\n- `sudo -l`\n- SUID / capabilities\n- cron/systemd jobs\n- writable scripts/directories\n- processes + service configs\n- shell history / dotfiles / keys\n- localhost-only services\n- kernel/version only after easier paths\n- linPEAS as cross-check\n\nUse GTFOBins for allowed binaries.","x":5400,"y":1310,"width":1250,"height":760},
		{"id":"eae94175fb5fa5e7","type":"text","text":"## Windows local enumeration\n\n- token privileges (`whoami /priv`)\n- users/groups/admins\n- services + permissions\n- service binary/DLL hijack\n- unquoted service paths\n- scheduled tasks\n- PowerShell history / juicy files\n- installed software / patches\n- `SeImpersonatePrivilege` opportunities\n- winPEAS / PowerUp cross-check","x":5400,"y":2150,"width":1250,"height":780},
		{"id":"cf8232df0ebc6770","type":"text","text":"# Decision: root / SYSTEM / useful admin?\n\n**NO:** re-enumerate local host; check what you skipped, inspect configs/processes/tasks, and re-check creds.\n\n**YES:** harvest credentials and evidence.\n\n**Standalone machine:** you may also be ready for Proof.","x":5400,"y":3010,"width":1250,"height":660,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"af96552e1a27881f","type":"text","text":"### PEN-200 sources\n\n[[PEN200_Chapter_16_Windows_Privilege_Escalation_Obsidian_Summary]]\n[[PEN200_Chapter_17_Linux_Privilege_Escalation_OSCP_Notes]]\n\nChapter 24 example: `sudo git` + pager escape → root.","x":5400,"y":3750,"width":1250,"height":560,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"16e2ac3cbba616f9","type":"text","text":"## Windows / AD credential sources\n\nWith sufficient privilege:\n- LSASS cached credentials (`Mimikatz`)\n- Kerberos tickets\n- local SAM / secrets\n- service-account credentials\n- domain hashes via DCSync once rights allow\n\nCredentials are often the bridge from **local compromise → lateral movement / AD**.","x":7200,"y":3030,"width":1250,"height":720},
		{"id":"c5af372cd959b807","type":"text","text":"# Decision: what did credentials reveal?\n\n**New account/host access** → re-enumerate that host/service.\n\n**Second subnet / internal-only services** → Pivoting.\n\n**Domain account / domain-joined host** → AD enumeration.\n\n**Standalone + required proof already obtained** → Proof.","x":7200,"y":3830,"width":1250,"height":700,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"4c71b0b6ade26662","type":"text","text":"# 5. CREDENTIALS\n\n**Goal:** turn local access into reusable identity.\n\nPasswords, hashes, keys, tokens, tickets, config secrets, Git history, browser/app/service credentials.","x":7200,"y":0,"width":1250,"height":460,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"f9493698602aaa74","type":"text","text":"## Search where applications/users leave secrets\n\n- app configs (`wp-config.php`, DB strings, `.env`)\n- scripts / backups\n- Git history (`git log`, `git show`)\n- SSH keys / authorized keys\n- PowerShell history\n- browser/app/password-manager files\n- service configs\n- world-readable sensitive files\n\nDeleted from working tree ≠ deleted from Git history.","x":7200,"y":560,"width":1250,"height":780},
		{"id":"a146cdbb21268e9a","type":"text","text":"# PEN-200 chapter map used in this canvas\n\n**Ch.6** Information Gathering → Recon / service enumeration\n**Ch.7** Vulnerability Scanning → Enumeration validation\n**Ch.8–10** Web + SQLi → Web enumeration / Initial Access\n**Ch.11** Client-side → Recon / phishing foothold\n**Ch.12** AV Evasion → payload delivery helper\n**Ch.13** Password Attacks → Credentials / Initial Access\n**Ch.14–15** Fixing + locating exploits → Enumeration → Initial Access\n**Ch.16–17** Windows/Linux PE → Privilege Escalation\n**Ch.18–19** Port forwarding / DPI tunnels → Pivoting\n**Ch.20** Metasploit → cross-stage helper, especially payloads/post-exploitation/pivoting\n**Ch.21–23** AD enum/auth/lateral movement → AD\n**Ch.24** Assembling the Pieces → end-to-end model","x":3600,"y":4900,"width":7200,"height":700,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"0aa3c75b3ae6d399","type":"text","text":"### PEN-200 sources\n\n[[6_Information_Gathering]] · [[PEN200_Chapter_07_Vulnerability_Scanning_Obsidian_Summary]] · [[PEN200_Chapter_08_Introduction_to_Web_Application_Attacks_OSCP_Notes]] · [[PEN200_Chapter_15_Locating_Public_Exploits_OSCP_Obsidian]]\n\n**Chapter 7 reminder:** automated scanning supports manual enumeration; it does not replace it.","x":1800,"y":4160,"width":1250,"height":570,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"c5183fa6a453e1e5","type":"text","text":"# Decision: credible attack path?\n\n**YES → Initial Access**\n\n**NO → go deeper**\n- different service\n- vhost/API/content discovery\n- credentials/defaults\n- exact version research\n- UDP\n- revisit notes and cross-correlate findings","x":1800,"y":3475,"width":1250,"height":620,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"2774962283e59f9c","type":"text","text":"## Cross-cutting helpers\n\n**AV evasion (Ch.12):** on-disk vs in-memory, shellcode/injection, test signatures.\n\n**Metasploit (Ch.20):** exploit modules, handlers, Meterpreter, payload generation.\n\nUse helpers when they solve a real blocker; keep the underlying technique understandable.","x":3600,"y":4120,"width":1250,"height":610,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"d7338de11e95ffb9","type":"text","text":"## DNS / naming clues\n\n- `host`, `nslookup`\n- DNSRecon / DNSenum\n- MX / TXT / A records\n- reverse lookups\n- subdomain brute force\n\nNames often reveal **roles**: DC, mail, web, database, backup, dev, staging.","x":0,"y":1360,"width":1250,"height":570},
		{"id":"e3e438f58df858b9","type":"text","text":"## Passive / supplied-domain recon\n\nUse when a domain/company context exists:\n- WHOIS\n- search operators / GHDB\n- GitHub / leaked code or secrets\n- Shodan\n- Netcraft\n- TLS / headers\n\nOSCP standalone targets often start directly with active recon.","x":0,"y":2010,"width":1250,"height":610},
		{"id":"5cdba1970e346b0e","type":"text","text":"### PEN-200 sources\n\n[[6_Information_Gathering]]\n[[PEN200_Chapter_11_Client-side_Attacks_Obsidian_Summary]] (target recon)\n\n**Carry forward:** IPs, names, ports, versions, likely OS, domains, and service roles.","x":0,"y":2720,"width":1250,"height":500,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"66eb22d1769d4281","type":"text","text":"## Active discovery — always do this\n\n```bash\nnmap <IP>\nnmap -p- <IP>\nnmap -sV -p <ports> <IP>\nsudo nmap -sU --open <IP>\n```\n\nAlso consider `-sC`, `-A`, NSE scripts, and host discovery when a subnet is in scope.\n\n**Do not stop at the first interesting port.**","x":0,"y":560,"width":1250,"height":720},
		{"id":"ea2b2676c28c0db2","type":"text","text":"# 🎯 RECEIVED TARGET IP\n\n**Start a host worksheet immediately**\n- Target / hostname / domain\n- Ports + services + versions\n- Users / credentials / hashes\n- Web paths (link/admin) / vhosts(admin.link) / APIs\n- Internal IPs / routes / DNS\n- Shells / privilege context\n- Evidence / proof\n\n**Rule:** every new fact can unlock an earlier dead end.","x":-1450,"y":0,"width":900,"height":620,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"5b4bae58736589a6","type":"text","text":"# 1. RECON\n\n**Goal:** discover reachable attack surface before committing to an exploit.\n\nStart broad, then narrow. Save output.","x":0,"y":0,"width":1250,"height":430,"color":"5"}
	],
	"edges":[
		{"id":"11d51632eeeedfcb","fromNode":"ea2b2676c28c0db2","fromSide":"right","toNode":"5b4bae58736589a6","toSide":"left","color":"4","label":"begin"},
